# Immediate reaction to a radio interface being plugged in, unplugged or reset.
#
# igate-watchdog.timer already checks every minute, which is enough to recover
# but long enough to notice. This rule asks for the same check the moment a USB
# sound card appears, so a replug is usually recovered before the next beacon is
# due. It matches `add` only: systemd cannot start a unit from the removal of
# the device it is keyed to, and an unplugged radio is the one case where the
# watchdog waits rather than acting anyway.
#
# The rule only starts the check; deploy_igate.sh watchdog decides whether
# anything is actually wrong, so it is harmless on a machine with other sound
# cards, and it does nothing at all until the gateway has been started once (it
# takes no action without run/wanted). That is why there is no filter here for
# the radio's own device: an earlier version matched ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb", which
# is set in the udev database but not in the property set while these rules are
# being applied, so the rule never fired. Matching every sound card costs one
# cheap check on a card that is not the radio, and works.
#
# Install it on the machine the interface plugs into:
#
#   sudo cp udev/99-igate-watchdog.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#
# It needs igate-watchdog.service, which the Raspberry Pi image installs. On a
# machine without that unit the rule does nothing.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="card*", \
  TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="igate-watchdog.service"
